+# Full independent review: Kourovka 21.9
+
+PASS for all five scoped results. The original free-pro-p finite-family question remains unsolved after five author turns. No historical novelty or full resolution is certified.
+
+## Source and scope
+
+Read the exact source gate and all five complete proofs. Visually checked October 2026 Kourovka printed page 177: 21.9 is unannotated and quantifies nonabelian free pro-p groups of finite rank and finite families of open subgroups including F. Checked Barnea–Ershov–Le Boudec–Reid–Vannacci–Weigel, arXiv:2507.04120v3, Question 4 on page 53 and its distinction from finitely many individual automorphisms. The preceding discrete-free analogue is explicitly not known in the free-pro-p setting. No result about the commensurator's simple quotients replaces the requested family.
+
+The credited pro-p basis, Schreier and Frattini facts, including Proposition 3.10 and Theorem 3.11, were checked on printed page 18. The natural surjection onto GL_d(Z_p) is stated in Notation 7.8. Nikolov–Segal Theorem 1.1 on printed page 172 was visually checked: topological finite generation gives strong completeness; the pro-p special case is credited there to Serre. These deep source theorems are dependencies, not newly proved results.
+
+## Turn 1
+
+The invariant-subgroup lemma does not assume the conclusion's closedness. Transvections with arbitrary p-adic entries extract every coordinate multiple, and permutations move it into each coordinate. The resulting set is an ideal of Z_p, hence zero or p^a Z_p by minimal valuation. Rank at least two is retained.
+
+The actual images of K, rather than only their closures, are therefore scalar lattices or zero. Equality M_F=j(M_U) gives the nonscalar inclusion contradiction exactly. The index-p Schreier basis has one p-scaled coordinate and repeated unscaled coordinates, so the image lattice is genuinely nonscalar. Primitive-power exclusions use torsion-free p-adic abelianization and do not assume a nonclosed subgroup is closed under arbitrary powers. Aut(F)-transitivity proves redundancy of additional index-p subgroups, not triviality of the common core.
+
+## Turn 2
+
+Strong completeness makes every abstract automorphism of each finite-rank ambient group continuous. Taking closure consequently preserves all required characteristicities and containment in the open subgroups. This legitimately reconciles the source's unrestricted subgroup wording with a closed-core formulation.
+
+Each characteristic-core operator is a finite intersection because the automorphism orbit lies among finitely many fixed-index open subgroups. The cyclic iteration's limit is characteristic in every U_i by a cofinal subsequence immediately after its operator is applied. A single cycle is not assumed to retain every earlier invariance. Maximality follows inductively for any common characteristic subgroup.
+
+The finite-step computation uses a Frattini quotient containing the coset kernel. The stated exponential bound on its finite p-group image suffices because successive nontrivial Frattini subgroups strictly decrease. Freeness and the Frattini basis criterion lift every automorphism of the specific quotient G/Phi^e(G). This is not asserted for arbitrary finite quotients. The suggested finite coset construction gives an in-principle finite step, not a decision procedure terminating on the infinite problem.
+
+The first two cores are exactly Phi(F) and Phi(U). Strict index growth follows because equality after a full cycle would produce an open common characteristic subgroup, excluded by turn 1. The packet correctly refuses to infer trivial intersection from index growth. Its cofinality equivalence uses compact decreasing sets outside each open Frattini neighborhood, and preserves the forall-depth/exists-stage order of quantifiers.
+
+## Turn 3
+
+Conjugation cyclically shifts the Schreier block in U_ab; the wraparound is inner conjugation by t^p and is therefore trivial on this abelianization. The words give T^(n-1)e_0, including the correct one-step indexing.
+
+The augmentation ideal is saturated, and the norm operator vanishes on it. Expanding (1+T)^j gives T^(p-1)=pB there. Modulo p, B is minus the identity plus a nilpotent polynomial, hence invertible; it preserves primitive vectors. Writing k-1=a(p-1)+r leaves T^(r+1)e_0 with degree at most p-1 and leading coefficient one. This establishes the exact coefficient valuation, including p=2. The finite-modulus threshold has the correct offset n>=e(p-1)+2.
