+The explicit words exclude containment of an entire finite lower-central term in K and exclude finite-class nilpotent quotients. They do not exclude all nontrivial characteristic subgroups. The other series are excluded only by their primitive powers. No universal claim about every derived-series term is introduced.
+
+## Turn 4
+
+The pro-p Hall theorem is applied to the closed finitely generated container H, not to the possibly infinitely generated normal subgroup N. Infinite index makes the complementary free factor nontrivial. The retraction forces equality of a and bab^-1 if both lie in H; the finite P wr C_p quotient separates these actual elements. Its existence uses the free-pro-p universal property and a nonidentity finite image of a, with no unsupported abstract normal-form assertion.
